    "recommendations": "• Install reflective address markers at both driveway entrances so responders can identify the correct entry point from the road.\n• Mark which driveway entrance is preferred for emergency vehicles to avoid confusion during an incident.\n• Trim trees and shrubs along the circular driveway so overhead and side clearance is maintained for fire apparatus and large emergency vehicles.\n• Cut back dense vegetation near the perimeter fencing to keep fence line gates and openings visible and reachable by responders.\n• Keep any gates in the perimeter fence unlocked or equipped with a Knox-Box or combination lock so responders can reach the rear of the property without delay.\n• Post a simple property map at the primary driveway entrance showing the location of the house, alternate access points, and any rear areas that are difficult to reach.\n• Clear a path along at least one side of the residence to give responders a walking route from the front driveway to the rear yard.\n• Keep the walkway to the main entrance free of planters, landscaping features, or other objects that could slow responder movement.\n• Confirm the circular driveway surface can support the weight of a fire engine and that no decorative edging or landscaping extends into the travel path."
